From: Cargt <tim.carney@cargt.com>
Subject: [PATCH] usb: typec: tcpci: unmask FAULT/VBUS_DISCNCT/etc alerts

tcpci_irq() clears every ALERT status bit it sees (the write-back a
few lines above tcpci_irq()'s return covers anything other than
RX_STATUS, unconditionally), but only reports IRQ_HANDLED for bits
that are also present in tcpci->alert_mask:

  return IRQ_RETVAL(status & tcpci->alert_mask);

tcpci_start() never includes TCPC_ALERT_FAULT, TCPC_ALERT_VBUS_DISCNCT,
TCPC_ALERT_RX_BUF_OVF, TCPC_ALERT_V_ALARM_LO or TCPC_ALERT_V_ALARM_HI
in that mask. A host actively negotiating USB-PD (voltage/current
renegotiation, VBUS transitions, marginal-cable fault conditions) can
legitimately assert any of these. Each occurrence is correctly
acknowledged at the register level, but never counted as "handled" by
this driver, so a host that triggers one of them repeatedly produces a
long run of IRQ_NONE returns from the kernel's point of view. Enough
of those in a short window and the generic IRQ layer's spurious-IRQ
protection disables the line outright ("irq N: nobody cared"), after
which the TCPCI stops reporting any further port events for the rest
of uptime - CC status, VBUS, everything - not just the alert type that
triggered the storm.

Observed on a PTN5110 (drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/tcpci.c handles it
directly, no vendor-specific quirk file) when booted with the USB-C
cable attached to a PD-capable host PC used for flashing; reproduced
consistently in that configuration, not seen with the cable
disconnected at boot or with a plain power-only source.

No new handling logic is added for these alert types - they're already
being cleared correctly by the existing unconditional write-back above
this mask check. This only fixes the driver's own bookkeeping so a
legitimate, already-serviced alert is recognized as such instead of
silently falling through and eventually taking the IRQ line down.

Signed-off-by: Cargt <tim.carney@cargt.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/tcpci.c b/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/tcpci.c
index 1111111111..2222222222 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/tcpci.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/tcpci.c
@@ -639,7 +639,18 @@

 	reg = TCPC_ALERT_TX_SUCCESS | TCPC_ALERT_TX_FAILED |
 		TCPC_ALERT_TX_DISCARDED | TCPC_ALERT_RX_STATUS |
-		TCPC_ALERT_RX_HARD_RST | TCPC_ALERT_CC_STATUS;
+		TCPC_ALERT_RX_HARD_RST | TCPC_ALERT_CC_STATUS |
+		/*
+		 * These are legitimately raised by a host doing active
+		 * USB-PD negotiation and are already cleared by the
+		 * unconditional write-back in tcpci_irq() regardless of
+		 * this mask - include them so that clearing is also
+		 * recognized as IRQ_HANDLED, instead of eventually
+		 * tripping the spurious-IRQ disable on this line.
+		 */
+		TCPC_ALERT_FAULT | TCPC_ALERT_VBUS_DISCNCT |
+		TCPC_ALERT_RX_BUF_OVF | TCPC_ALERT_V_ALARM_LO |
+		TCPC_ALERT_V_ALARM_HI;
 	if (tcpci->controls_vbus)
 		reg |= TCPC_ALERT_POWER_STATUS;
 	/* Enable VSAFE0V status interrupt when detecting VSAFE0V is supported */
